Help on class dAeDFM in module dAeDFM:

class dAeDFM(builtins.object)
 |  dAeDFM(
 |      len_learn_window=None,
 |      var_order=None,
 |      max_forecast_step=1,
 |      target_type='Original',
 |      fwd_cumsum=False
 |  )
 |
 |  Nonlinear Dynamic Factor Model by Deep Autoencoder
 |  (dAeDFM), or, Deep Autoencoder of Nonlinear Dynamic
 |  Factor Model: Nonlinear dynamic factor model (DFM)
 |  formulated as deep autoencoder (DAE) composed of
 |  dense/causal nowcasting/forecasting sub-layers and
 |  using robust squared-L1 loss criterion
 |
 |  Acronyms
 |  --------
 |
 |  DFM : dynamic factor model of multiple (or vector)
 |      time-series
 |  AE : autoencoder (a structure of neural networks)
 |  DAE : deep AE (with multiple latent layers)
 |  dAeDFM : nonlinear dynamic factor model by deep
 |      autoencoder, or, deep autoencoder of nonlinear
 |      dynamic factor model
 |
 |  dAeDFM Modeling
 |  ---------------
 |
 |  * By nonlinearization of well-established dynamic
 |    factor models (DFM) on large sets of multiple
 |    time-series, we introduce nonlinear DFM formulated
 |    as deep autoencoders (DAE) composed of sub-layers
 |    of dense nowcasting, dense forecasting, causal
 |    nowcasting and causal forecasting.
 |
 |  * Different DAE configurations mixing the dense/
 |    causal nowcasting/forecasting layers can make
 |    the nonlinear DAE model serving as differently
 |    as total nowcasting, predictive nowcasting,
 |    one-step forecasting, enhanced forecasting
 |    and total forecasting models.
 |
 |  * To resist outliers in input data (other than
 |    those in output-target difference), we introduce
 |    robust squared-L1 loss criterion. Our squared-L1
 |    loss function having both outlier-resistant
 |    robustness of L1 loss (mean absolute error)
 |    and continuous differentiability of L2 loss
 |    (mean squared error).
 |
 |  * In addition, separate validation and testing
 |    datasets can be formed for nonlinear model
 |    training and evaluation using our observation-
 |    based input data sample variation approach.
 |
 |  Why deep autoencoder (DAE)
 |  --------------------------
 |
 |  * neural networks introduce nonlinearity by applying
 |    certain elementwise nonlinear activation functions
 |    to linear combinations (weighted sums)
 |
 |  * multi-layered (deep) neural nets add more
 |    nonlinearity by nested (elementwise) nonlinear
 |    functions
 |
 |  * neural networks with latent layer of hidden
 |    neurons are validated by proven and widely
 |    accepted Universal Approximation Theorem
 |
 |  * autoencoders (a specific structure of neural nets)
 |    were initially formulated as nonlinear extensions
 |    of linear principal components analysis (PCA)
 |
 |  * both time-domain PCA and frequency-domain
 |    (spectral) PCA are among core methods to
 |    formulate linear DFM models (Ling, 2026)
 |
 |  * underlying the current AI boom worldwide, hugely
 |    successful Large Language Models (LLMs) are made
 |    of transformers (recently invented structures
 |    of deep neural networks)
 |
 |  Linear DFM
 |  ----------
 |
 |  Observed vector (i.e. multiple) time-series
 |      y(t) : observed zero-mean time-series of n-by-1
 |             column data vector y with time index t
 |      n : number of individual time-series
 |
 |  Unobserved factor score time-series
 |      f(t) : unobserved time-series of m-by-1 factor
 |             score column vector f with time index t
 |      m : number of factors: factor score time-series
 |
 |  Size of DFM
 |      p : vector autoregressive (VAR) order of DFM,
 |          p >= 1
 |
 |  Parameter values
 |      t = 0, 1, 2, ..., T
 |      T : last time-stamp
 |      n >> 1
 |      m >= 1
 |      m < n or m << n
 |
 |  Measurement (observation) equation of linear DFM
 |      y(t) = X(0) f(t) + X(1) f(t-1) + ... + X(p) f(t-p) + u(t)
 |
 |  State transition equation of linear DFM
 |      f(t) = A(1) f(t-1) + A(2) f(t-2) + ... + A(p) f(t-p) + v(t)
 |
 |  Filter equation of linear DFM
 |      f(t) = S(0) y(t) + S(1) y(t-1) + ... + S(p) y(t-p) + w(t)
 |
 |  Equivalent representations of linear DFM
 |      (1) Classic representation: by
 |          (1.1) measurement (observation) equation
 |          (1.2) state transisiton equation
 |      (2) An equivalent representation: by
 |          (2.1) measurement equation
 |          (2.2) filter equation
 |
 |  Estimated linear DFM coefficients
 |      X(j) : n-by-m matrix of factor loadings
 |      A(j) : m-by-m matrix of factor VAR coefficients
 |      S(j) : m-by-n matrix of filter coefficients
 |
 |  Time-series of random vectors
 |      u(t) : idiosyncratic component vector
 |      v(t) : prediction error vector
 |      w(t) : fiter error vector
 |
 |  Basic (neural network) autoencoder
 |  ----------------------------------
 |
 |  Components of basic neural network feed-forward function
 |          based on coding convention in PyTorch
 |      x_obsvd : observed data ROW vector
 |      y : unobserved hidden code ROW vector
 |      W1 : linear weight matrix of len(y)-by-len(x)
 |      b1 : bias row vector of len(y)
 |      W2 : linear weight matrix of len(x)-by-len(y)
 |      b2 : bias row vector of len(x)
 |      x_recon : reconstructed data ROW vector
 |      g(*) : element-wise nonlinear activation function
 |
 |  Baisc neural network autoencoder
 |      encoder :  y = g( x_obsvd W1^T + b1 )
 |      decoder :  x_recon = g( y W2^T + b2 )
 |
 |  Simplest AE of Nonlinear DFM
 |  ----------------------------
 |
 |  Symmetric autoencoder (AE) of Nonlinear DFM
 |      with single hidden layer of latent codes
 |
 |  Layer ID
 |      data input/output layer ID: i = 0
 |      latent code (factor) layer ID: i = 1
 |
 |  Data layer ROW vector: 1-by-(n*(p+1))
 |      h(0) = [ h(0,0), h(0,1), ..., h(0,p) ]
 |           = [ y(t)^T, y(t-1)^T, ..., y(t-p)^T ]
 |
 |  Segments of data layer row vector
 |      h(0,k) = y(t-k)^T, k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Latent layer ROW vector, 1-by-(m*(p+1))
 |      h(1) = [ h(1,0), h(1,1), ..., h(1,p) ]
 |           = [ f(t)^T, f(t-1)^T, ...,  f(t-p)^T ]
 |
 |  Segments of latent layer row vector
 |      h(1,k) = f(t-k)^T, k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Example of element-wise nonlinear activation function
 |      g(x) = tanh(x)
 |
 |  Encoding layer function, based on DFM Filter equation
 |      h(1,k) = g( h(0,k) W(1,0,k,k)^T +
 |                + h(0,(k+1)) W(1,0,k,(k+1))^T +
 |                + ... +
 |                + h(0,p) W(1,0,k,p)^T )
 |          k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Decoding layer function, based on DFM Measurement equation
 |      h(0,k)^ = g( h(1,k) W(0,1,k,k)^T +
 |                 + h(1,(k+1)) W(0,1,k,(k+1))^T +
 |                 + ... +
 |                 + h(1,p) W(0,1,k,p)^T
 |          k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Causal layer: data availability Applied
 |      encoding weight:  W(1,0,k,j) = 0  for  all j < k
 |      decoding weight:  W(0,1,k,j) = 0  for  all j < k
 |
 |  Nowcasting layer:
 |      encoding weight:  W(1,0,k,0) NOT= 0  for  k = 0
 |      decoding weight:  W(0,1,k,0) NOT= 0  for  k = 0
 |
 |  Reconstructed data by AE
 |  ------------------------
 |
 |  Reconstructed data output by Linear decoder
 |      h(0,k)^ = h(1,k) W(0,1,k,k)^T +
 |              + h(1,(k+1)) W(0,1,k,(k+1))^T +
 |              + ... +
 |              + h(1,p) W(0,1,k,p)^T
 |          k = 0
 |
 |  Loss function (e.g. mean square error) for
 |          autoencoder backpropagation gradient descent
 |      y(t)^ = [h(0,0)^]^T
 |      Loss = MSE( h(0,0)^, h(0,0) ) = MSE( y(t)^, y(t) )
 |
 |  Simple AE with sub-layers
 |  -------------------------
 |
 |  Examples of asymmetric element-wise activation function
 |          based on built-in ReLU(x) function in PyTorch
 |      g(+)(x) = Max(0,x) = ReLU(x)
 |      g(-)(x) = Min(0,x) = (x)-ReLU(x)
 |
 |  Causal encoding sub-layer with bias b(+) and ReLU(x)
 |        h(+)(1,k) =
 |      = g(+)( h(0,k) W(+)(1,0,k,k)^T +
 |            + h(0,(k+1)) W(+)(1,0,k,(k+1))^T +
 |            + ... +
 |            + h(0,p) W(+)(1,0,k,p)^T + b(+)(1) )
 |      k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Causal encoding sub-layer with bias b(-) and (x)-ReLU(x)
 |        h(-)(1,k)
 |      = g(-)( h(0,k) W(-)(1,0,k,k)^T +
 |            + h(0,(k+1)) W(-)(1,0,k,(k+1))^T +
 |            + ... +
 |            + h(0,p) W(-)(1,0,k,p)^T + b(-)(1) )
 |      k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Causal sub-layers: data availability Applied
 |      W(+)(1,0,k,j) = 0  for  all j < k
 |      W(-)(1,0,k,j) = 0  for  all j < k
 |
 |  Nowcasting sub-layers:
 |      W(+)(1,0,k,0) NOT= 0  for  k = 0
 |      W(-)(1,0,k,0) NOT= 0  for  k = 0
 |
 |  Causal encoding layer feed forward function
 |      h(1,k) = h(+)(1,k) + h(-)(1,k)
 |          k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Entire output row vector on causal encoding layer
 |      h(1) = [ h(1,0), h(1,1), ..., h(1,k), ..., h(1,p) ]
 |
 |  Causal decoding sub-layers
 |      same fead-forward structure as causal encoding sub-layers
 |
 |  Dense (vs. causal) layer
 |  ------------------------
 |
 |  Basic neural network feed-forward function
 |      "Dense layer": Feeding forward ALL data points
 |
 |  Dense encoding sub-layer with bias b(+) and ReLU(x)
 |        h(+)(1,k)
 |      = g(+)( h(0,0) W(+)(1,0,k,0)^T +
 |            + h(0,1) W(+)(1,0,k,1)^T +
 |            + ... +
 |            + h(0,p) W(+)(1,0,k,p)^T + b(+)(1) )
 |      k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Dense encoding sub-layer with bias b(-) and (x)-ReLU(x)
 |        h(-)(1,k)
 |      = g(-)( h(0,0) W(-)(1,0,k,0)^T +
 |            + h(0,1) W(-)(1,0,k,1)^T +
 |            + ... +
 |            + h(0,p) W(-)(1,0,k,p)^T + b(-)(1) )
 |      k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Dense sub-laters: ignoreing causality of
 |      data availability in time dimension
 |
 |  Nowcasting sub-layers:
 |      W(+)(1,0,k,0) NOT= 0
 |      W(-)(1,0,k,0) NOT= 0
 |
 |  Dense encoding layer feed forward function
 |      h(1,k) = h(+)(1,k) + h(-)(1,k)
 |          k = 0, 1, ..., p
 |
 |  Entire output row vector on dense encoding layer
 |      h(1) = [ h(1,0), h(1,1), ..., h(1,k), ..., h(1,p) ]
 |
 |  Dense decoding sub-layers
 |      same fead-forward structure as dense encoding sub-layers
 |
 |  Forecasting (vs. nowcating) layer
 |  ---------------------------------
 |
 |  Data availability for 1-step forecast
 |      W(+)(1,0,k,0) = 0
 |      W(-)(1,0,k,0) = 0
 |
 |  Dense forecasting sub-layer with ReLU(x)
 |      SET  W(+)(1,0,k,0) = 0  for  ALL k
 |
 |  Dense forecasting sub-layer with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |      SET  W(-)(1,0,k,0) = 0  for  ALL k
 |
 |  Causal forecasting sub-layer with ReLU(x)
 |      SET  W(+)(1,0,k,0) = 0  for  k = 0
 |
 |  Causal forecasting sub-layer with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |      SET  W(-)(1,0,k,0) = 0  for  k = 0
 |
 |  Types of layers in AE of nonlinear DFM
 |  --------------------------------------
 |
 |  Four types of encoding or decoding layers
 |      (1) nonlinear nowcasting layer
 |      (2) nonlinear forecasting layer
 |      (3) dense linear nowcasting layer
 |      (4) dense linear forecastign layer
 |
 |  Each nonlinear layer is composed of sub-layers
 |
 |  Eight types of nonlinear sub-layers
 |      (a) dense or causal
 |      (b) nowcasting or forecasting
 |      (c) with ReLU(x) or (x)-ReLU(x)
 |
 |  Each nonlinear nowcasting layer aggregates four sub-layers
 |      dense nowcasting with ReLU(x)
 |      dense nowcasting with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |      causal nowcasting with ReLU(x)
 |      causal nowcasting with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |
 |  Each nonlinear forecasting layer aggregates four sub-layers
 |      dense forecasting with ReLU(x)
 |      dense forecasting with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |      causal forecasting with ReLU(x)
 |      causal forecasting with (x)-ReLU(x)
 |
 |  Deep AE of nonlinear DFM
 |  ------------------------
 |
 |  Structure
 |      (1) A symmetric deep autoencoder
 |      (2) Generating hidden code by dense linear
 |          layer as the last encoding layer
 |      (3) Reconstructing observed data by dense
 |          linear layer as the last decoding layer
 |      (4) Having one or more nonlinear encoding layers
 |      (5) Having one or more nonlinear decoding layers
 |
 |  Number of latent layers
 |      Two or more latent encoding layers
 |      Two or more latent decoding layers
 |
 |  Example of deep AE of nonlinear DFM
 |  -----------------------------------
 |
 |  Number of layers
 |      Three latent encoding layers
 |      Three latent decoding layers
 |
 |  Layer IDs of this Symmetric DAE of nonlinear DFM
 |      Data layer:
 |          ID = 0
 |      Latent layers:
 |          ID = 1  for  first encoding and last decoding layers
 |          ID = 2  for  middle encoding and middle decoding layers
 |          ID = 3  for  last encoding and first decoding layers
 |
 |  Number of observed data time-series
 |      n = m(0)
 |
 |  Number of latent variable time-series
 |      m(0) > m(1) > m(2) > m(3)
 |
 |  Number of hidden code (unobserved factor) time-series
 |      m = m(3)
 |
 |  Dimension (size) of row vector on each layer (ID = i)
 |      d(i) = m(i) * (p + 1)
 |          i = 0, 1, 2, 3
 |
 |  Three pairs of encoding layers
 |          (using coding convention in PyTorch)
 |      from d(0) to d(1) : observed data input
 |      from d(1) to d(2)
 |      form d(2) to d(3) : hidden code output
 |
 |  Three pairs of decoding layers
 |      from d(3) to d(2)
 |      from d(2) to d(1)
 |      from d(1) to d(0) : reconstructed data output
 |
 |  Four pairs of nonlinear layers
 |      from d(0) to d(1) : encoding
 |      from d(1) to d(2) : encoding
 |      from d(3) to d(2) : decoding
 |      from d(2) to d(1) : decoding
 |
 |  Two paris of dense linear layers
 |      form d(2) to d(3) : generating hidden code
 |      from d(1) to d(0) : generating reconstructed data
 |
 |  Nowcasting/forecasting models
 |  -----------------------------
 |
 |  Total nowcasting model
 |      All (en- & de-coding) layers: Nowcasting Only
 |
 |  Predictive nowcasting model
 |      First (data input) encoding layer: Nowcasting
 |      At least one other layer: Forecasting
 |
 |  One-step forecasting model
 |      First (data input) encoding layer: Forecasting
 |      All other layers: Nowcasting Only
 |
 |  Enhanced forecasting model
 |      First encoding layer: Forecasting
 |      One or more other layer: Nowcasting
 |      One or more other layer: Forecasting
 |
 |  Total forecasting model
 |      All (en- & de-coding) layers: Forecasting Only
 |
 |  Choice of Loss Criterion
 |  ------------------------
 |
 |  Following are available options of loss functions
 |      having smooth backpropagation gradients
 |
 |  MSE (L2) Loss
 |      the most utilized loss criterion
 |      built-in in PyTorch
 |
 |  Smooth L1 Loss
 |      resisting outliers of large output-target difference
 |      built-in in PyTorch
 |
 |  Huber Loss
 |      resisting outliers of large output-target difference
 |      built-in in PyTorch
 |
 |  Squared-L1 Loss
 |      resisting outliers of extreme input data value
 |      developed for DAE of nonlinear DFM
 |
 |  Evaluation datasets
 |  -------------------
 |
 |  Traditional datasets for model validation/test
 |      A portion of vector time-series set aside,
 |          i.e. NOT used in model training
 |
 |  Evaluating NON-linear models (e.g. dAeDFM)
 |      Constructing evaluation or test vector
 |          time-series z(t) BASED on input vector
 |          time-series y(t)
 |
 |  Validation or test vector time-series
 |      z(t) = y(t) + a y(t1) + [-a/(1+a)] y(t2)
 |
 |  Time-stamps t1 and t2
 |      Different from t and from each other
 |      Chosen randomly
 |
 |  "Input data sample variation rate"
 |      Coefficient a (> 0)
 |
 |  Examples
 |      a = 0.20 for validation dataset
 |      a = 0.25 for test dataset
 |
